{"attempt_id": "attempt:um-ep-38-erd-s-problem-38-92706992-source-fidelity-01", "created_at": "2026-09-06T08:00:00Z", "graph_id": "graph:um-ep-38-erd-s-problem-38-92706992-v1", "obligations": [{"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof", "counterexample", "formalization", "computation"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": ["obligation:um-ep-38-erd-s-problem-38-92706992-statement-fidelity"], "kind": "root_claim", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-ep-38-erd-s-problem-38-92706992-root", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/EP-38"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Does there exist $B\\subset\\mathbb{N}$ which is not an additive basis, but is such that for every set $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$ of Schnirelmann density $\\alpha$ and every $N$ there exists $b\\in B$ such that $ \\lvert (A\\cup (A+b))\\cap \\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\rvert\\geq (\\alpha+f(\\alpha))N $ where $f(\\alpha)>0$ for $0<\\alpha <1 $? The Schnirelmann density is defined by $ d_s(A) = \\inf_{N\\geq 1}\\frac{\\lvert A\\cap\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\rvert}{N}. $"}, "statement_sha256": "5ceb603bea1c1587a71f95a682b266a5b4b453d526732ede387bd23fafd6f086"}, {"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": [], "kind": "definition", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-ep-38-erd-s-problem-38-92706992-statement-fidelity", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/EP-38"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Verify against the cited source that the frozen statement is complete, current, untruncated, attribution-correct, and unambiguous enough for mathematical research; record any definition or quantifier gap without silently editing the contract."}, "statement_sha256": "65f0b3d574bd6d837403149dab6434c0f571709f7eb5bb066c7e8774bad13f34"}], "problem_contract_sha256": "80ff85b544eb7c9e370e763b13c